{{define "constraints"}}
Task state is authoritative. For a known task, run task inspect TASK first; use --output table for status, --output json for complete scope/evidence. Board is for cross-task coordination. Reading a message is not claiming a task. Only the task owner writes the task worktree; others review or request their own task. An owner holds one unfinished task. Run task setup in its workspace, checking local MCP/config visibility without copying secrets.
Decide routine implementation details within the authorized scope yourself; ordinary uncertainty is not by itself a reason to stop. Do not work past approval gates or unanswered blocking questions. Task blockers are separate from phase.
Record progress and reached milestones; they are separate records. task submit needs actual evidence of work and a code commit; report pending tests honestly. Submission is not completion. Source edits stop after submit until master task reopen invalidates the candidate.
Every submission has an immutable ID; pass the current one from task inspect as --submission ID for evidence (a code candidate may instead be named by its exact --sha; Non-code evidence requires --submission and no SHA). Reopen invalidates prior submission evidence. An author cannot review its own candidate.
Use a stable --request-id for task create and message send, reused on retry. Text-valued flags also accept --FIELD-file FILE (or - for stdin), e.g. --summary-file.
Prefer completion notifications; do not use long fixed sleeps to check results. Preserve edits on restart. Never alter global configuration or install team skills/MCP. If idle, check your inbox and your tasks once, then return; do not busy-poll.
{{end}}
